// Package startup is the platform entry/startup glue.  It is the startup
// counterpart to `rt`'s runtime *services* (alloc/refcount/exit): `rt` is
// services, `startup` is startup.  It is force-included in every hosted link
// (via cmd/bnc's ensureRuntimeDepsLoaded) so the startup/entry glue can live
// here in Binate rather than in runtime/binate_runtime.c.
//
// It holds the command-line-argument seam — `Args()` and `SetArgs`, which
// `pkg/std/os` re-exports (os.Args/os.SetArgs delegate here; os imports startup)
// — and, on a hosted program, the process entry itself: a Binate `main`
// (args_main.bn's `_entry`, `#[c_export("main")]`) that captures the real argv and
// installs it via SetArgs, then hands off to bn_entry, replacing the C `main` that
// lived in runtime/binate_runtime.c.  The backing `args` cell (unexported, in
// args.bn) has no initializer, so package __init cannot clobber what `_entry`
// installed before it ran; a baremetal image (entrypoint "start") and a --library
// build (entrypoint "init") have no entry that installs argv, so os.Args() is
// empty for them until a caller sets one via os.SetArgs.  See design-ffi-export.md
// §3.3, plan-build-version-predicate.md, and plan-ffi-export-detailed.md Phase 6.
//
// Still to come (design-ffi-export.md §3.3): a first-class baremetal `_start`
// written in Binate (today it is crt0.s) and a C-library `_init` entry.
package "pkg/builtins/startup"

// Args returns the program's command-line arguments, indexed like Go's os.Args:
// element 0 the program name, elements 1.. the arguments.  Fully readonly (the
// slice and its argument text), so it is safe to share the same slice with every
// reader.  Empty until an entry installs argv via SetArgs (the hosted `_entry`
// does, from the real argv).  os.Args() delegates here.
func Args() @[]readonly @[]readonly char

// SetArgs replaces the arguments Args() returns and returns the PREVIOUS value, so
// they can be saved and restored: `old := SetArgs(new); …; SetArgs(old)` (the
// returned value flows straight back in).  newArgs is the whole argv — element 0
// the program name, 1.. the arguments — so the caller controls index 0.  It is the
// seam a bytecode interpreter (cmd/bni) uses to give an interpreted program its own
// argv (via os.SetArgs, since os is injected host-native), and the hosted entry
// uses it to install the real argv — see explorations/design-os-args-vm.md.
func SetArgs(newArgs @[]readonly @[]readonly char) @[]readonly @[]readonly char

// Env returns the process environment as "NAME=value" strings (POSIX environ
// order), a fully-readonly managed slice safe to share.  os.Env() delegates here.
// The snapshot the hosted entry installed from envp, or whatever SetEnv last
// installed — empty on a target with no entry to install one.  (The backing `env`
// global is unexported; read it through this accessor.)
func Env() @[]readonly @[]readonly char

// SetEnv replaces the environment Env() returns and returns the PREVIOUS value,
// so it can be saved and restored: `old := SetEnv(new); …; SetEnv(old)` (the
// returned value flows straight back in).  Each entry is a "NAME=value" string.
// The env counterpart of SetArgs — the seam a bytecode interpreter (cmd/bni)
// uses to give an interpreted program its own environment (via os.SetEnv, since
// os is injected host-native) — see explorations/design-os-args-vm.md.
func SetEnv(newEnv @[]readonly @[]readonly char) @[]readonly @[]readonly char
